    "content": "A rare tie occurred at the 2026 Oscars in the Best Live Action Short Film category between \"The Singers\" and \"Two People Exchanging Saliva,\" marking only the seventh tie in Academy history. Ties occur when nominees receive the exact same number of votes. This video shows the moment the tie was announced at the 2026 Oscars:00:40ABC7YouTube • Mar 15, 2026 00:40 History of Ties at the Oscars 2026: Best Live Action Short Film: The Singers and Two People Exchanging Saliva. 2013: Best Sound Editing: Skyfall and Zero Dark Thirty. 1995: Best Live Action Short Film: Franz Kafka's It's a Wonderful Life and Trevor. 1987: Best Feature Documentary: Artie Shaw: Time Is All You've Got and Down and Out in America. 1969: Best Actress: Katherine Hepburn (The Lion in Winter) and Barbra Streisand (Funny Girl). 1950: Best Documentary Short: A Chance to Live and So Much for So Little. 1932: Best Actor: Fredric March (Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde) and Wallace Beery (The Champ). Note: At the time, regulations stated a nominee within three votes of the winner also received an Oscar. How a Tie WorksTies occur when two, or more, nominees receive the exact same number of votes. When this happens, both participants receive an award, causing a slight disruption to the ceremony, as noted by presenter Kumail Nanjiani during the 2026 ceremony, who jokingly described the \"shocking\" scenario as necessitating that the award take \"twice as long\".",
